## DARE-001: Eliminate vendor submodules via josh

### Decision — Perdurant

```toml
[decision]
what = """
Replace 20 vendor/ git submodules with josh-proxy filtered projections
from a single unified vendor history. Each vendor/X becomes a josh
filter ::vendor/X/ — cloned once, projected infinitely. Agents never
run git submodule commands again.
"""
who = "b00t-core (operator approval required per PAIR-CONCEPT)"
when = "post-#527 merge, pre-#516 MCP action layer"
scope = [
    "vendor/ tree (20 submodules → 20 filtered projections)",
    "CI pipeline (remove git submodule update --init --recursive)",
    "agent sandboxing (read-only per-agent projections via josh proxy)",
    "justfile (new vendor-sync, vendor-status recipes)",
]
explicitly_out_of_scope = [
    "b00t-cli/ crate (not a submodule)",
    "b00t-mcp/ crate (not a submodule)",
    "crates/ufo-types (not a submodule)",
    "Full monorepo migration (b00tyverse stays multi-repo outside vendor/)",
]
```

### Alternative — Abstract

```toml
[[alternatives]]
name = "Do nothing — keep 20 submodules"
viable = "Zero migration cost; zero risk"
rejected_because = """
Agent safety surface compounds: agents hallucinate git submodule commands
that corrupt state. CI time grows with each submodule init. Submodule drift
between branches creates invisible coupling. The 20-submodule status quo
is a measurable drag on every b00t operation.
"""

[[alternatives]]
name = "git-subrepo — merge externals into single repo"
viable = "No external tool dependency; history preserved in-repo"
rejected_because = """
Loses upstream remote tracking. Manual resync burden when upstreams change.
21k+ commits in vendor tree would bloat b00t history. josh's filter approach
keeps upstreams live while presenting unified view.
"""

[[alternatives]]
name = "git-filter-repo — one-time split/merge"
viable = "Well-maintained Python tool; used by large orgs"
rejected_because = """
One-time operation, not ongoing. Doesn't solve the agent safety problem
(live git commands still dangerous). No proxy layer for read-only agent
projections. Requires re-running on every upstream change.
"""

[[alternatives]]
name = "GitButler but CLI only — no josh backend"
viable = "Agent-friendly undoable git; already packaged"
rejected_because = """
GitButler solves the UX layer but not the submodule problem. Without josh
as the history backend, gitbutler still sees 20 separate repos. The two
are complementary: josh for history/projection, gitbutler for agent CLI.
"""
```

### Risk — Moment

```toml
[[risks]]
name = "josh project maturity"
severity = "HIGH"
description = "josh has ~1 active maintainer, 1.9k stars, 77 forks. Project could go unmaintained."
mitigation = """
- Pin vendor/josh to known-good SHA in workspace config
- Keep .gitmodules as fallback for 90-day transition period
- b00t controls the proxy layer — can swap josh for alternative
  without changing agent interface (just vendor-sync recipe stays)
- If josh dies, revert to submodules is a single commit
"""

[[risks]]
name = "Agent retraining failure"
severity = "HIGH"
description = "Agents trained on git submodule commands may hallucinate them post-migration."
mitigation = """
- New DARE-JOSH-AGENT-PROTOCOL datum codifies agent rules
- just vendor-sync wraps ALL vendor operations — agents never touch josh CLI
- Gate: block-git-submodule-commands added to pre-commit hook
- Training: fine-tune corpus includes josh workspace syntax examples
- RHAI guard: if agent runs 'git submodule', auto-respond with correct josh command
"""

[[risks]]
name = "CI pipeline breakage"
severity = "MEDIUM"
description = "All workflows use git submodule update --init --recursive today."
mitigation = """
- Replace with josh proxy startup step (docker compose service)
- Run both submodule and josh pipelines for 2-week overlap
- CI gate: compare checkout between submodule and josh paths — must be identical
"""

[[risks]]
name = "Vendor contribution workflow friction"
severity = "MEDIUM"
description = "Contributing upstream changes to vendor repos becomes different."
mitigation = """
- josh-link maintains bidirectional sync with upstreams
- just vendor-contribute <vendor> recipe: open PR against upstream
- Upstream changes flow in via just vendor-sync (no submodule bump commits)
"""

[[risks]]
name = "Operator unfamiliarity with josh"
severity = "LOW"
description = "Operator has not used josh before. Learning curve exists."
mitigation = """
- DARE documents provide complete context
- just vendor-status shows projection health at a glance
- josh-agent-protocol datum provides quick-reference for common operations
- Fallback to submodules always available
"""
```

### Endurant — Endurant

```toml
[endurant]
# What will exist in the codebase after adoption

artifacts = [
    # Core config
    "vendor.josh — declarative workspace file defining all 20 vendor projections
     with upstream URLs, pinned revs, and filter paths",

    # Just recipes (agents use these, not raw josh)
    "just vendor-sync — update all vendor projections to pinned revs
     (replaces git submodule update --init --recursive)",
    "just vendor-status — show projection health: rev, upstream, drift, dirty",
    "just vendor-contribute <name> — open PR against upstream for a vendor change",

    # Agent safety
    "_b00t_/datums/josh-agent-protocol.tomllmd — agent-safe josh instruction set:
     what agents MAY do, MUST NOT do, and recovery procedures",
    "_b00t_/scripts/josh-recovery.rhai — RHAI script: restore projection X
     to known-good rev after agent hallucination",

    # CI gates
    "gate: validate-vendor-workspace — CI gate checking vendor.josh parseability
     and projection reachability",
    "gate: vendor-drift-check — CI gate comparing all vendor/ trees against
     pinned revs, blocking on unexpected drift",

    # Training corpus
    "fine-tune/corpus/josh-agent-examples.jsonl — training examples showing
     correct agent interactions with josh-backed vendor tree",
]

removed = [
    # These files/patterns disappear from the codebase
    ".gitmodules (all 20 vendor entries removed; b00t-* entries stay)",
    "All vendor/ gitlinks from git index (git rm --cached vendor/*)",
    "git submodule update --init --recursive from all CI workflow YAMLs",
    "git submodule update --remote from all just recipes",
    "Submodule drift warnings from pre-commit hook (no longer applicable)",
]

# How to verify the DARE is fulfilled
acceptance_criteria = [
    "vendor.josh exists and is parseable by josh-compose",
    "just vendor-sync succeeds and all 20 projections are at pinned revs",
    "CI passes without any git submodule commands",
    "Agent protocol datum is loaded by b00t learn josh-agent-protocol",
    "Gate validate-vendor-workspace passes in CI",
    "All removed artifacts are confirmed absent from the codebase",
]
```

### Transition Plan

```
Week 1-2:  vendor.josh created, josh proxy running in CI alongside submodules
Week 3:    Agent protocol datum published, training examples in corpus
Week 4:    Submodule gitlinks removed, CI switched to josh-only
Week 5-12: Fallback .gitmodules kept (read-only), agents retrained
Week 13:   Fallback removed, DARE verified against acceptance criteria
```

